A||||5||Gold-86SE||Starter Bolt Problems||||9||
Z||000000||Gold-86SE||11-30-2000||09:45 PM||||<BR>Ok guys, hope you can help me with this small yet major (or at least appears major) problem.<P>I changed the Duke in my 84 and my new block has a broken starter bolt. The starter bolt that is broken is the bolt closest to the block, which is the long bolt.<P>Question is how do you think I should proceed.<P>1) Operate with only one bolt, which I  really don't want to do.<P>2) Try to extract the bolt.<P>3) Make some type of make-shift bolt.<P><BR>Need your suggestions, thanks for the help.<BR><P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Cold and Rain Daily Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)||||reg||9
Z||000001||RNGCRSH||11-30-2000||09:58 PM||||There is always a right way and a wrong way.<BR>choices 1 & 3 are the wrong way. pick #2.  Extracting a bolt is a pain in the A**, but you will be glad in the long run when your starter does not fall off the motor.<P><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>------------------<BR>"Fear" The sand in the machinery of life.<p>[This message has been edited by RNGCRSH (edited 11-30-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000002||Seanh||11-30-2000||10:29 PM||||They make some backwards threaded screws to extract broken bolts. They worked great for me, although mine was just on the thermostat housing, not the starter. You just get a center punch and make an indintation in the top of the bolt and use a tiny drill bit to drill down the middle of it, then put in the back-threaded extractor and tighten it until the bolt starts comming out. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tigger||11-30-2000||10:49 PM||||I'm going on my instinct on this one<P>Do 2)<P>1) = Chewed up flywheel.<P>3) = Well not sure how you can rig up something for this one.<P>Go with what Seanh is saying.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||PlasticWrapd||12-01-2000||12:57 AM||||I had the exact same problem.  I extracted the bolt using a small drift.  I sharpened the drift to a point and used a small hammer to tap the outer edges of the bolt and SLOWLY unscrew it.  Soak the area with PB Blaster first.  Took about an hour.  <P>when you install the new bolt, don't overtighten it.  This stretches the bolt and weakens it.  I used threadlocker and torqued it to 30 ft/lbs.  So far, so good.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Gold-86SE||12-01-2000||09:26 AM||||<BR>Thanks for the info.<P>Now for the work, I'll let you know how it comes out - no pun intended.<BR>||||reg||1
